// Updates: no chips (short, single-purpose list). Browse and Installed: the
// Kind chips. The view controls sit on the same row, inside the webview
// rather than the title bar: the title bar folds them into `…` on a narrow
// sidebar, and the feature glossary's Show me can only pulse what it owns.

// Update takes precedence on the one row button for a direct (non-bundle)
// outdated install, then Complete Install; via-bundle rows always show the
// Bundle nav button (its update path stays in the gear), and rows with nothing
// outstanding show Uninstall.

// `grim status` carries no registry attribution (its own `source` field
// means direct-vs-via-bundle), so an installed row would fall back to
// longest-configured-prefix — which reaches NO alias when the configured
// entries are indexes, and the Installed tree then roots on the bare host
// while Browse roots on the alias. The browse catalog holds grim's own
// attribution for the very same repo; borrow it. An artifact absent from
// the catalog keeps the fallback, as it must.

// An empty message is the user declining the disclosure — nothing
// happened and nothing needs saying. Everything else is a real failure,
// and NOTHING is recorded for it: the state stays exactly as it was.

// Same repo re-clicked (e.g. the second click of a double-click, now that
// the sidebar posts single-clicks without a disambiguation delay): the
// content is already painted — just reveal, no repaint/revalidate churn.

/** Statistic producer named by the index's `providers.rating`. Deliberately a
 *  plain string, not an enum: the value is index-supplied and an unrecognised
 *  one degrades to "readable, not writable". */

/** Commits early only when a debounce is actually pending — a blur that lands
 *  after the timer already fired (and already committed) is a no-op, so it
 *  doesn't resend an identical value. */

// PIPE NOTHING. Falling through to a token-less `grim rate` would make
// grim run its own credential ladder against a host we could not
// authenticate against; the honest answer is that we have no credential.
// The reason rides along so the caller can offer the way out.

/** Server-side body inlined into #root so the first HTML parse shows structure
 *  before the script runs (details skeleton). Trusted markup — callers pass
 *  already-escaped render output. Empty for the sidebar. */

